    content: `Under the Indian Patents Act, 1970 (as amended), Section 3(p) expressly bars patenting of "an invention which, in effect, is traditional knowledge or which is an aggregation or duplication of known properties of traditionally known component or components." This is one of the most critical provisions for Ayurveda. A classical Ayurvedic formulation described in authoritative texts (such as those listed in the First Schedule of the Drugs & Cosmetics Act) is considered traditional knowledge and is NOT patentable under Indian law. However, genuinely novel modifications, new delivery systems, or novel combinations that demonstrate inventive step beyond the traditional knowledge may still be patentable. The burden is on the applicant to show that the invention is not merely traditional knowledge. The Indian Patent Office routinely consults the Traditional Knowledge Digital Library (TKDL) to check prior art based on traditional knowledge.`,
