    content: `WHO Traditional Medicine Strategy provides the international framework. KEY ELEMENTS: (1) OBJECTIVES: Integrate traditional medicine (TM) into health systems, promote safety/efficacy/quality, increase access; (2) REGULATION: WHO recommends national regulatory frameworks for TM products; encourages monograph development; supports pharmacovigilance for TM; (3) QUALITY: WHO Guidelines on Good Agricultural and Collection Practices (GACP) for medicinal plants; WHO Guidelines on GMP for herbal medicines; (4) SAFETY: WHO Guidelines on safety monitoring of herbal medicines; (5) INTEGRATION: WHO encourages integration of TM into primary healthcare; (6) IP IMPLICATIONS: WHO recognizes need to protect TK from misappropriation; supports documentation of TK; encourages benefit-sharing; (7) WHO HERBAL MONOGRAPHS: WHO publishes monographs for medicinal plants — useful as quality references and can serve as evidence for safety/traditional use in regulatory submissions; (8) INTERNATIONAL REGULATORY COOPERATION (IRC): WHO facilitates cooperation between national regulatory authorities for TM products; (9) ICD-11: International Classification of Diseases now includes a Traditional Medicine module.`,
