    category: "guidance",
    jurisdiction: "international",
    ipType: "patent",
    content: `STRATEGIC GUIDE FOR INTERNATIONAL PATENTING OF AYURVEDIC INNOVATIONS: (1) PATENTABILITY LANDSCAPE: India (strict — Section 3(p), 3(d) bars for TK); US (more receptive — no specific TK bar, but 35 USC 102/103 novelty/obviousness apply); EU (EPO receptive to novel herbal compositions with data); Japan (JPO receptive, especially with pharmacological data); China (CNIPA — growing acceptance of TCM/herbal patents). (2) WHAT'S PATENTABLE ABROAD: Novel extraction methods, novel drug delivery systems (nanoformulations, liposomal, transdermal), synergistic combinations with pharmacological evidence, new therapeutic indications with clinical data, bioavailability-enhanced formulations, standardized extracts with novel quality markers. (3) FILING STRATEGY: India first filing → PCT application → Selective national phase entry. Consider provisional patent filing for early priority date. (4) DISCLOSURE CONSIDERATIONS: WIPO GRATK Treaty (2024) will require GR/TK disclosure globally once implemented. Proactively disclose to strengthen application. (5) COSTS: PCT + national phase in 5 countries: approximately Rs. 20-40 lakh over 3-5 years. Startups can use government schemes (SIPP, Patent Facilitation Program) for subsidies. (6) TKDL IMPACT: TKDL agreements with foreign patent offices mean classical formulations will be found as prior art. Focus claims on truly novel aspects.`,
