    category: "rule",
    jurisdiction: "international",
    ipType: "regulatory",
    content: `Australia's Therapeutic Goods Administration (TGA) regulates Ayurvedic products as Complementary Medicines. TWO PATHWAYS: (1) LISTED MEDICINE (AUST L): Lower risk, self-assessed for quality/safety. Must comply with TGO 102 (quality standards). Permitted ingredients only (listed in ARTG). No pre-approval clinical trials. Manufacturing must comply with cGMP (PIC/S). Can make limited indications from approved list. (2) REGISTERED MEDICINE (AUST R): Higher risk or higher-level claims. Requires full evaluation including clinical evidence. Much more expensive and time-consuming. IMPORT REQUIREMENTS: Products must be manufactured at TGA-approved or MRA-recognized facilities. Indian GMP (Schedule T) may not be sufficient — PIC/S GMP required. Heavy metals limits: arsenic <5 ppm, cadmium <0.3 ppm, lead <10 ppm, mercury <0.5 ppm. Sponsor must be an Australian entity.`,
