    category: "statute",
    jurisdiction: "india",
    ipType: "patent",
    content: `Section 25 of the Patents Act provides for opposition to patent grant. (1) PRE-GRANT OPPOSITION (Section 25(1)): Any person can file after patent application is published but before grant. Grounds include: (a) wrongly obtained invention; (b) prior publication; (c) prior claiming; (d) prior public knowledge/use in India; (e) obviousness; (f) not an invention under Section 3 (including 3(p) for TK); (g) insufficient description; (h) non-disclosure of information under Section 8; (i) convention application not filed within 12 months. (2) POST-GRANT OPPOSITION (Section 25(2)): Any interested person within 12 months of publication of grant. Same grounds. SIGNIFICANCE FOR AYURVEDA: This is the primary mechanism to challenge wrongful patents on traditional knowledge. TKDL-based evidence can be submitted in opposition. NGOs, government bodies, and industry associations can file pre-grant opposition to prevent biopiracy patents. Process: File Form 7A with grounds and evidence → Controller constitutes Opposition Board → Hearing → Decision.`,
